data: {"delta":{"partial_json":"{\"file_path\": \"/home/<USER:1>/.local/share/cs-campaign/output/report.md\", \"content\": \"# Campaign report: greet, tested\\n\\n## Mission\\nDeliver `src/greet.py` with `greet(name)` returning `\\\"Hello, \u003cname\u003e!\\\"` and\\n`tests/test_greet.py` covering a normal name and the empty string, with\\n`python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` exiting 0.\\n\\n## What happened\\n- Dispatched the full mission to `dev` (dispatch d002) in a single pass, since\\n  the mission is small and self-contained.\\n- `dev` implemented `src/greet.py` (`greet(name)` returns\\n  `\\\"Hello, {}!\\\".format(name)`) and `tests/test_greet.py` (two `unittest`\\n  cases: a normal name and the empty string), and committed to its own branch\\n  `cs-sandbox/<DEV:1>.<GROUP:1>` at commit `57f62c0`.\\n- Did not trust the reported result: fetched `dev`'s branch, inspected the\\n  diff directly, checked out the commit in a scratch worktree, and ran\\n  `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` myself.\\n  - Result: `Ran 2 tests in 0.000s / OK`, exit code `0`.\\n- All three mission criteria confirmed independently. Accepted d002.\\n\\n## Verdict\\nAll stated criteria are satisfied. Outcome: campaign-met.\\n\\n## Where the work lives\\nThe implementation is on `dev`'s branch\\n(`cs-sandbox/<DEV:1>.<GROUP:1>`, commit `57f62c0`), not on the\\norchestrator's branch — the orchestrator's role was to dispatch and judge, not\\nwrite code, per `orchestrator.md`.\\n\"}","type":"input_json_delta"},"index":0,"type":"content_block_delta"}

